{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1092", "sample_idx": 45, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "My Japan Trip in 300 Words\n\nI arrived in Kyoto just as cherry blossoms began to fall—pink petals dusting the streets like confetti from a quiet celebration. I wandered through Fushimi Inari’s endless red torii gates, each one a silent prayer, and lost myself in the hush between footsteps. At a tiny tea house tucked behind a bamboo grove, an elderly woman served matcha with such reverence I felt I was being handed a piece of history.\n\nTokyo was a symphony of neon and noise. In Shibuya, I stood at the scramble crossing, swept along by a river of people—no one bumped, no one yelled. In Akihabara, I watched a girl in a cat-eared hoodie argue gently with a robot vending machine. In Shinjuku, I ate yakitori under string lights, sipping cold beer as rain tapped the pavement.\n\nI took the bullet train to Hiroshima. The Peace Memorial Park was still. A single origami crane, folded by a child, sat on the monument. I added mine—a small, crooked thing—and whispered thanks.\n\nIn Nara, deer bowed for snacks, their eyes knowing, their antlers brushing my palm. At a ryokan in Hakone, I soaked in an onsen under stars, steam curling like ghosts around me. The owner brought a plate of yuzu mochi—sweet, tart, perfect.\n\nJapan doesn’t shout. It whispers. It leaves you with a quiet awe, a full heart, and the urge to return.\n\nI didn’t buy much. But I brought home silence. And the taste of matcha on my tongue.\n\nThree weeks. One country. A lifetime of stillness in motion.\n\n— A traveler who learned to breathe again", "logprobs": [{"token": "My", "logprob": -0.23346616327762604}, {"token": " Japan", "logprob": -0.2209511399269104}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -0.09506416320800781}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -0.31332796812057495}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.002152984729036689}, {"token": "3", "logprob": -0.0005376085755415261}, {"token": "0", "logprob": 